# Agent Operating Rules

Scope: Next.js (TypeScript) frontend, Sanity CMS, inert Strapi archive, Vercel.
Goal: Keep agents aligned, predictable, and low-overhead.

## 1. Authority & Scope

- Each agent acts only within its domain
- Cross-domain changes require an explicit handoff note (what changed, why, impact)
- No agent unilaterally changes architecture, auth model, or data contracts

## 2. Default Response Format

Every agent response follows this structure:

```
Intent — what the agent is trying to achieve
Assumptions — environment, constraints, inferred requirements
Plan — ordered steps
File-level changes — exact paths, create/modify/delete
Risks — failure modes + mitigation
```

## 3. Change Discipline

- Prefer additive changes over refactors
- Refactors require a stated payoff (bug fix, perf win, simplification)
- No "cleanup", "while I'm here", or stylistic rewrites

## 4. Contracts Over Convenience

All boundaries are explicit:
- Frontend ↔ Sanity API
- Server ↔ client components
- Build ↔ runtime configuration

Contracts are documented in code (types, schemas, adapters), not prose.
No ad-hoc fetches or inline magic strings for endpoints or env vars.

## 5. Type Safety (Hard Rule)

- No `any`, no unchecked external data
- External inputs (Sanity responses/webhooks/env vars) validated at boundary
- App-internal code may assume validated types

## 6. Determinism & Reversibility

- Changes must be reproducible from repo + env vars alone
- Deploy-impacting changes include: rollback note, failure symptom
- Migrations/config changes are forward-only and safe to re-run

## 7. Environment Separation

- Local, preview, and production are treated as distinct
- No agent assumes production behavior from local defaults
- Secrets never cross environment boundaries

## 8. Minimal Surface Area

- Introduce the smallest abstraction that solves the problem
- New libraries require justification: what it replaces, why native tools are insufficient

## 9. Observability by Default

Non-trivial logic changes include:
- Where failures surface (logs, UI, metrics)
- How to confirm success post-deploy
- Silent failure is considered a bug

## 10. Completion Definition

A task is complete only when:
- Acceptance criteria are explicitly satisfied
- Side effects are listed
- Follow-ups are named but not executed

## 11. Prohibited Behaviors

- Implicit architectural drift
- Style-only edits
- Assumptive security changes
- Broad refactors without scope control
- "Trust me" decisions without tradeoffs

## 12. Canonical Handoff Note

Use between agents or for cross-domain changes:

```
What changed:
Why it changed:
Files touched:
Contract impact:
Deployment impact:
Risks:
```
